  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 339 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper trough within Canada is spreading its influence over our\nregion, with a feed of relatively dry, continental air mass\ninfiltrating into the region. Surface high pressure settles into\nour area amid this dry, cooler air mass. With clear skies and\npredominately calm winds, ideal radiational cooling will allow\nfor temperatures to dip into the mid 30s to upper 20s \ntonight/Friday morning. This will result in widespread frost and\nareas of freezing temperatures. Mid level clouds are forecast to\nspread into our area from the west, but expected to occur near\nsunrise still allowing for frost/freeze. Urban landscapes may\nnot see frost/freezing temperatures due to locally warmer\ntemperatures from urban heat island effects.\n\nAdditional chances for frost exist this weekend into next week,\nparticularly Sunday night into Monday morning.\n\nThe upper pattern remains in a quasi-zonal flow through the weekend,\nwith a progressive shortwave trough forecast to move through \nFriday night into Saturday. This will provide forcing for \nascent bringing a medium chance for rain showers. This wave is \nlikely to be devoid of overall moisture content, thus rain \namounts are anticipated to be rather low - less than quarter of \nan inch. Rain may come in two bouts - the first Friday night \nassociated with warm air advection and low level frontogenesis, \nwith the second bout more during the day Saturday associated \nwith the passage of the mid level wave and associated cold\nfront.\n\nEnsemble guidance suggests in transient shortwave ridging early\nnext week, although the influence of this remains in question.\nThere could be a brief trend in temperatures toward slightly\nabove average temperatures; otherwise, temperatures are forecast\nto remain near average.",
